{
  "assessment": "The change introduces a new `median()` function in `cohort/stats.py` that computes the middle index using integer division (`n // 2`). This logic bypasses the requirement to average the two central values when the dataset contains an even number of elements, directly contradicting the finance standard where [3000, 4200, 4400, 5000] must yield 4300. As written, any report feeding even-length cohorts into this function will produce systematically incorrect medians.\n\nDefects found:\n1. **Even-length median ignores averaging**\n   - `call`: `python -c \"from cohort.stats import median; print(median([3000, 4200, 4400, 5000]))\"`\n   - `expected`: 4300\n   - `actual`: 4400",
  "findings": [
    {
      "actual": "4400",
      "at": "cohort/stats.py:9",
      "call": "python -c \"from cohort.stats import median; print(median([3000, 4200, 4400, 5000]))\"",
      "defect": "Even-length median ignores averaging",
      "expected": "4300"
    }
  ]
}
